// The text of every House document in this bill's lineage, amendments included.
//
// Bundled rather than fetched: the General Court's API sends no CORS headers,
// so a browser cannot read it directly, and the text of a filed bill does not
// change once it is published.
//
// H.5349 is here with an empty string on purpose. It is a set of amendments to
// a Senate bill rather than a bill of its own, so the legislature publishes no
// text under that number; what the amendments produced was published as H.5366.
// That is a fact about the document rather than a gap in this data, and the
// reader should be told it rather than shown nothing.
//
// The floor amendments come from the Senate bill, not this one: the House was
// amending S.2581, so its thirty-one amendments hang off that document. They are
// House work on a House step all the same, which is why they are here.
//
// The amendment step carries three documents, because two questions are worth
// answering separately: what members asked for, and what the House actually
// adopted. "All 31 amendments" is every one as filed, with its sponsor and what
// became of it; the two consolidated amendments are the text that was voted on.
// Amendment 30 is named rather than reprinted there: it is the text as amended,
// identical to H.5366, and printing it twice would bury the rest.
//
// `stage` ties a document to the step of the lineage it belongs to, so choosing
// one in the picker moves the stepper and choosing a step moves the picker.

export interface BillText {
  number: string;
  title: string;
  /** The lineage step this document belongs to. */
  stage: string;
  /** Empty where the legislature publishes none. */
  text: string;
  /**
   * Where a document has no text of its own, what it became. H.5349 is a set
   * of amendments to a Senate bill rather than a standalone text, and what
   * those amendments produced was published under this number.
   */
  publishedAs?: string;
  /** Why there is no text, in the record's own terms. */
  note?: string;
  /**
   * MAPLE's own subject tags for the document, each a category and a topic
   * within it. Only bills carry them: an amendment is not a bill, so the
   * amendment entries have none.
   */
  topics?: { category: string; topic: string }[];
}

